  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nA cold front is tracking southeastward across our forecast area\nat the time of this writing. Some pre-frontal convection that \ndeveloped and affected areas near and east of I-57 a little \nwhile ago has since moved off to our east into IWX's CWA. GOES \nsatellite imagery shows that cumulus in the trailing portion of \nthe same pre-frontal confluence zone is still attempting to \ngrow, and additional isolated to widely scattered shower and \nthunderstorm development could still in our far southeastern CWA\nover the next few hours. However, the convergence/confluence \nhere isn't quite as good as it was earlier farther to the north,\nso not anticipating any greater convective coverage in our CWA \nthrough the remainder of this afternoon.\n\nHeading into Halloween weekend, a couple of disturbances of \nPacific origin are expected to lift northeastward into the \nMidwest. While these disturbances are progged to remain somewhat\ndisorganized, their arrival -- paired with modest isentropic \nascent, broad synoptic forcing within the right entrance region \nof a 100+ kt upper- level jet streak over the Great Lakes, and \nan associated influx of tropospheric column moisture -- will \nresult in some showery/drizzly periods through the weekend.\n\nThe first raindrops could be seen in our forecast area as early\nas midday tomorrow, though with dry low-mid-level air to work \nthrough and the better forcing still lagging to our southwest, \nmuch of the area likely won't see any measurable rainfall until \nFriday night into Saturday morning as the more coherent forcing \nand better moisture spreads into the area. Couldn't rule out a \nfew isolated lightning strikes occurring during this Friday \nnight into Saturday morning time period as slightly steepened \nmid-level lapse rates attempt to creep into our CWA. However, \nforecast soundings indicate that most of any associated meager \ninstability should remain concentrated below the -20C level -- \nsuggesting that even the taller convective cells may struggle to\nachieve electrification. Deterministic and ensemble guidance \nbroadly suggests that the shower activity should tend to \ndecrease in coverage going into Halloween evening, though at \nthis time, it remains possible that at least isolated pockets of\nrain and/or drizzle could manage to hang around past sunset and\naffect trick-or-treating activities for some. Additional \nperiods of rain and/or drizzle may be seen in our area on Sunday\nas well, though forecast confidence in this is lower compared \nto Saturday.\n\nEarly next week, multiple additional upper-level disturbances \nare expected to conglomerate somewhere over the western or \ncentral CONUS and yield a slow-moving, high-amplitude composite \nlongwave trough. The exact details of how everything will come \ntogether remains unclear at this time, but it's likely that \nwe'll see additional rainy periods next week as this troughing \nmeanders towards and across our longitude. Temperatures for next\nweek will also largely hinge on the evolution of this trough \nand when any associated rainfall works its way through our area.\n\nOgorek",
